{0 Getting started}

This page takes you from an empty directory to a running GTK 4 window
written in OCaml: install the libraries, set up a dune project, write a
small counter application, and build and run it. It ends with pointers to
the rest of the documentation.

You need OCaml 4.14 or later, opam and dune, and a basic grasp of OCaml. You
don't need to know GTK's C API.

{1 Install}

The packages are not in opam-repository yet, so install them from a clone of
the repository. First install GTK 4's development files:

{@sh[
sudo apt-get install libgtk-4-dev     # Debian, Ubuntu
sudo dnf install gtk4-devel           # Fedora
brew install gtk4                     # macOS (Homebrew)
]}

Then pin the packages and install [ocgtk]:

{@sh[
git clone https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk.git
cd ocgtk
for p in conf-*/; do opam pin add -n -y "${p%/}" "./$p"; done
opam pin add -n -y .
opam install ocgtk
]}

The [conf-*] packages check that the C libraries are installed. Installing
[ocgtk] also installs the packages it depends on, such as [ocgio] and
[ocgdk] (see {{!page-conventions.section-libraries}Libraries and namespaces}).

{1 Create a project}

Create a directory with three files:

{@text[
counter/
  dune-project
  dune
  counter.ml
]}

[dune-project]:

{@dune[
(lang dune 3.0)
]}

[dune]:

{@dune[
(executable
 (name counter)
 (libraries ocgtk))
]}

[counter.ml]:

{@ocaml[
open Ocgtk

let activate app =
  let window = Application_window.new_ app in
  window#set_title (Some "Counter");
  window#set_default_size 300 200;

  let count = ref 0 in
  let label = Label.new_ (Some "Clicked 0 times") in
  let button = Button.new_with_label "Click me" in
  ignore
    (button#on_clicked
       ~callback:(fun () ->
         incr count;
         label#set_label (Printf.sprintf "Clicked %d times" !count))
       ());

  let box = Box.new_ `VERTICAL 12 in
  box#set_margin_top 12;
  box#set_margin_bottom 12;
  box#set_margin_start 12;
  box#set_margin_end 12;
  box#append (label :> Widget.widget_t);
  box#append (button :> Widget.widget_t);

  window#set_child (Some (box :> Widget.widget_t));
  window#present ()

let () =
  let app = Application.new_ (Some "org.example.Counter") [ `DEFAULT_FLAGS ] in
  ignore (app#on_activate ~callback:(fun () -> activate app) ());
  exit (app#run 0 None)
]}

{1 Build and run}

{@sh[
dune build
dune exec ./counter.exe
]}

A window opens with a label and a button. Each click updates the label.
Closing the window ends the program.

{1 What the program does}

- {b The application.} {!Ocgtk.Application} starts GTK, runs the main loop,
  and quits when its last window closes. GTK emits its [activate] signal
  when the application starts, and that is where the window is built. The
  ID [org.example.Counter] names the application to the desktop. See
  {{!page-conventions.section-mainloop}The main loop and threads}.
- {b Creating objects.} Each GTK class has a module ({!Ocgtk.Window},
  {!Ocgtk.Button}, ...) with its constructors: [Button.new_with_label]
  wraps [gtk_button_new_with_label]. A constructor returns an OCaml object,
  and you call its methods with [#]: [window#set_title].
- {b Upcasting.} [Box#append] and [Window#set_child] take any widget, as a
  [Widget.widget_t]. OCaml does not convert a [button_t] to a [widget_t] on
  its own, so the code upcasts with [:>]. See
  {{!page-conventions.section-upcasting}Upcasting}.
- {b Signals.} [button#on_clicked ~callback ()] connects a handler to the
  button's [clicked] signal. Don't leave out the final [()]: without it the
  handler is never connected. See {{!page-conventions.section-signals}Signals}.
- {b Optional values.} Where the C API accepts [NULL], the OCaml binding
  takes an option, such as [Some "Counter"] for the title.

{1 Next steps}

- {{!page-conventions}The conventions page} explains how the bindings are
  organised: the two layers of each library, the anatomy of a class module,
  inheritance and casting, properties, memory management and how C types
  map onto OCaml.
- {{!page-modules}The module index} lists every GTK class, interface and
  record. Each module's documentation comes from GTK's own, so the upstream
  {{:https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/}GTK 4 documentation} reads the same way.
- The {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/tree/main/ocgtk/examples}examples}
  include a text editor, a settings panel, a login form and a calculator.
